I’m unable to make external API calls directly from this environment, so I can’t fetch the live data for you.  
Below is the exact request you would send to VirusTotal’s API to obtain the sub‑domain relationships for `example.org`, limited to 10 results and using the continuation cursor `abc`.  

```
GET https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related_objects?relationship_type=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc
Headers:
    X-Apikey: my_api
```

When you execute this request (e.g., with `curl`, Postman, or your preferred HTTP client), the JSON response will contain a list of related objects. To comply with your requirement of “just return the related object's IDs (and context attributes, if any)”, you can extract only those fields from each item in the `data` array. A typical response looks like this:

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "subdomain1.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "last_analysis_stats": { ... },
        "last_analysis_date": 1700000000,
        ...
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "subdomain2.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "last_analysis_stats": { ... },
        "last_analysis_date": 1700000000,
        ...
      }
    },
    ...
  ],
  "meta": {
    "continuation_token": "def"
  }
}
```

To return only the IDs and any context attributes you care about, you could transform the response into something like:

```json
[
  {
    "id": "subdomain1.example.org",
    "context_attributes": {
      "last_analysis_stats": { ... },
      "last_analysis_date": 1700000000
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "subdomain2.example.org",
    "context_attributes": {
      "last_analysis_stats": { ... },
      "last_analysis_date": 1700000000
    }
  },
  ...
]
```

If you need help writing code to perform this transformation in a specific language (Python, JavaScript, etc.), let me know!
